# @public — sadish premultiplied writers: a surface with REAL alpha, for agnos gpu_shader_op#92.
#
# premul.cyr — every other writer in sadish treats an SdSurface as an OPAQUE render target: the
# coverage blits (sd_canvas_blit_at, sd_canvas_blit_paint_at) and the blend (sd_blend_hline) force
# the alpha byte to 255, and sd_clear cannot even say "transparent" (an alpha byte of 0 means OPAQUE
# under the legacy rule, see surface.cyr). Correct for a screen; useless for a client whose surface
# is flagged SETU_SURF_PREMULTIPLIED, which the compositor blends per pixel with #92 op 0x01
# (`out = src + dst * (1 - src_a)`, setu proto.cyr / aethersafha gpu.cyr). Until this module no
# sadish writer could produce such a surface with a non-255 alpha — no rounded corners, soft
# shadows or translucent panels over a transparent window. These are the writers that can:
#
#   sd_clear_premul(s, color, a)                      # every pixel := color at alpha a (0 = 0,0,0,0)
#   sd_fill_rect_premul(s, x, y, w, h, color, a)      # premultiplied src-over of a rect, clipped
#   sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, s, color, a, dx, dy) # coverage x colour, src-over, clipped
#   sd_canvas_blit_premul(cv, s, color, a)            # ... with the canvas at the surface origin
#   sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at(cv, s, g, dx, dy)  # coverage x GRADIENT paint (0.7.0, see below)
#   sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul(cv, s, g)             # ... with the canvas at the surface origin
#   sd_surface_rgba_at(s, x, y)                       # raw 0xAARRGGBB readback, no alpha mapping
#
# THE CONTRACT.
#   · The DESTINATION is premultiplied BGRA (every channel <= its pixel's alpha) and is read as it
#     is: nothing here unpremultiplies, and nothing maps an alpha byte. A fresh sd_surface_new is
#     all-zero = transparent, and a surface the legacy writers only ever painted with OPAQUE colours
#     (sd_clear, the blits, sd_fill_rect_blend) has alpha 255 everywhere — both valid. ⚠ Not valid:
#     raw 0x00RRGGBB stores (byte 3 = 0), and sd_hline / sd_plot with an sd_rgba colour, which store
#     STRAIGHT alpha. Compositing onto such a pixel cannot repair it.
#   · Every output pixel is VALID premultiplied (out_c <= out_a) for every valid destination,
#     proved below and swept in programs/premul_test.cyr. Every stored value is in [0, 255] even
#     over an invalid destination, so no store8 ever truncates.
#   · Rows are addressed by sd_surface_stride(s); only the w*4 visible bytes of a row are touched.
#   · Clear, fill and both blits allocate NOTHING, first call included (asserted: 0 B on the heap,
#     0 B on an arena hook) — there is no scratch; the gradient blit does not even allocate on the
#     first call that rebuilds the ramp (it is rebuilt in place). Single-threaded, like the rest of
#     sadish.
#
# ⛔⛔ ALPHA IS AN EXPLICIT ARGUMENT `a` (0..255, clamped) AND 0 MEANS TRANSPARENT — EVERYWHERE IN
# THIS MODULE, the blit included. The colour's own alpha byte is NEVER read: `color` supplies R, G, B.
# Why not the legacy sd_alpha_of rule (byte 0 = opaque) that sd_canvas_blit_at / paint use:
#   · #92 reads byte 3 LITERALLY — 0 is transparent to the kernel. A premultiplied writer that
#     read 0 as OPAQUE would put sadish and the compositor on opposite sides of the one value the
#     stack has already been burned by: a byte-3 misreading is what made kashi's glyph text and a
#     coverage-blend colour render as an ADDITIVE OVER-BRIGHT GHOST under #92 (dhancha canvas.cyr /
#     surface.cyr, aethersafha window.cyr W_PREMUL) — a wrong picture with no error anywhere.
#   · Under the legacy rule a COMPUTED alpha that reaches 0 (a fade-out's last frame) paints SOLID —
#     the sd_rgba(0, 0, 0, 128)-painted-black trap sd_fill_rect_blend was built to close in 0.5.3.
#   · The stack's colours arrive without alpha anyway: dhancha's theme tokens are 0x00RRGGBB with
#     their alpha in a separate token (dh_theme_scrim / dh_theme_scrim_a), which is this signature.
# ⚠ The price, pinned by the suite in both directions: sd_rgba(r, g, b, 128) passed with a = 255
# paints OPAQUE (byte ignored), and sd_rgb(r, g, b) passed with a = 0 paints NOTHING.
# ⛔ PORTING. sd_canvas_blit_at reads only R, G, B — coverage is its whole source alpha, whatever byte
# 3 holds — so its port is sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, s, c, 255, dx, dy): a = 255, NOT sd_alpha_of(c).
# sd_alpha_of would halve an sd_rgba(.., 128) colour that blit_at paints solid (0xFF80007F against
# blit_at's 0xFFFF0000 for red over opaque blue, pinned), and map a computed alpha that reached 0 back
# to 255 — the paints-solid trap above. A caller that means to START honouring a colour's alpha passes
# it raw: sd_color_a(c), where 0 is transparent.
#
# ================================================================
# THE GRADIENT PAINT — sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at(cv, s, g, dx, dy) (0.7.0).
# The combination a translucent UI actually needs: a gradient panel with anti-aliased rounded corners
# on a TRANSPARENT window. src/paint.cyr's SdGradient (linear / radial / focal radial, multi-stop,
# pad / repeat / reflect, gradient transforms) composited through a canvas's coverage — sampled,
# stepped and evaluated EXACTLY as sd_canvas_blit_paint_at does, and composited with the arithmetic
# below instead of paint's straight src-over-with-alpha-forced-255.
#
# ⭐ ONE EVALUATION, TWO COMPOSITORS. The two blits are the SAME function: paint.cyr's
# _sd_paint_blit_run / _sd_paint_blit_point take a `pm` flag that chooses the store, and nothing
# above the store differs — same DDA, same root, same spread, same 1025-entry ramp, same marked-cell
# stop search. So no pixel can take a different t or a different colour here than it takes there:
# for an OPAQUE gradient at full coverage the two blits write byte-identical RGB, asserted pixel by
# pixel over a non-trivial canvas in programs/paint_premul_test.cyr (group B).
#
# ⛔ NO `a` ARGUMENT, AND 0 STILL CANNOT MEAN OPAQUE. Every other writer here takes an explicit
# alpha because a sadish colour's byte 3 is ambiguous (the legacy 0-means-opaque rule). A gradient
# is not ambiguous: sd_gradient_add_stop resolves each stop through sd_alpha_of when the ramp is
# built, so a ramp entry's byte 3 is the ACTUAL interpolated alpha, 1..255, and this blit uses it as
# the source alpha directly. The byte-3 misreading that burned kashi cannot reach this path.
# ⚠ The price of the legacy rule, paid in paint.cyr and inherited here: a gradient CANNOT express a
# fully transparent stop — sd_rgba(r, g, b, 0) is an OPAQUE stop. The faintest expressible stop is
# alpha 1, which over transparent leaves out_a = 1 at full coverage and 0 below cov 128. A caller
# that wants a true fade to nothing ends the ramp at alpha 1 (or stops painting).
# ⇒ There is no alpha-0 no-op to skip: every covered pixel has k >= 1. The no-ops are the ones paint
# already has — coverage 0 (untouched), zero stops and a blocked matrix (nothing painted, SADISH_OK).
#
# ⭐ PREMULTIPLY AFTER INTERPOLATION, PER PIXEL — not into the ramp. The ramp interpolates STRAIGHT
# (non-premultiplied) colours, SVG's rule and paint.cyr's documented contract; this blit premultiplies
# the RESULT at each pixel, P_c = floor(c * a / 255), which is exactly sd_premul of that colour. Why
# not store premultiplied entries once and interpolate those:
#   · The premultiply is TRUNCATING (it has to be: P is then exactly sd_premul, the stack's
#     sanctioned producer — see the ⚠ REPEATED COMPOSITING note below). Truncating at the STOPS
#     throws the colour away at the faint end — P = 0 for every c < 255 at a = 1 — and every
#     interpolated value between the stops then carries that loss, pulling the whole transition
#     toward the opaque stop's colour and, where that stop is dark, toward BLACK.
#     MEASURED, stops opaque BLACK -> WHITE at alpha 1, the pixel at t = 0.5 over an opaque white
#     destination: straight-then-premultiply gives 191 per lane (colour 128 at alpha 128, P = 64),
#     premultiplied-interpolation 128 (P = 1) — 63 levels darker, on a ramp whose ends are identical
#     either way. ⚠ P = 1, not the 0 the truncation alone suggests: the premultiplied ends would be
#     (0,0,0,255) and (1,1,1,1) and _sd_gradient_lerp ROUNDS, so their midpoint comes back up to 1.
#     Pinned in both directions in programs/paint_premul_test.cyr (group K, checks 71-75).
#   · The ramp is SHARED with sd_canvas_blit_paint_at and with sd_gradient_color_at. A premultiplied
#     one would be a second 4,100 B table and a second answer to "the colour at t".
# ⇒ A ONE-STOP gradient of colour c at alpha a paints EXACTLY sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, s, c, a)'s
# bytes — the premultiplied twin of paint.cyr's "an opaque one-stop gradient paints sd_canvas_blit_at's
# bytes", and asserted the same way.
#
# ================================================================
# THE ARITHMETIC — chosen to equal agnos's own references, so a CPU-composited frame and the GPU
# path agree. Channels c in {B, G, R}; cov = coverage 0..255 (255 for clear/fill); d = destination.
# ⭐ ONE implementation, _sd_premul_cov_run, which both blits call; the gradient blit passes the
# PIXEL'S OWN interpolated colour and its own straight alpha as (c, a), and nothing else changes.
# ⚠ a is the pixel's alpha and cov its coverage, kept apart to the last division: the effective
# source alpha a * cov / 255 is never rounded to a byte on the way (the straight paint blit's
# `sa = coverage * paint_alpha / 255` does round it, and that 8-bit truncation is one of the two
# reasons the two blits differ below full coverage — the other is straight's truncating store).
#
#   P_c  = floor(c * a / 255)       the premultiplied colour — EXACTLY sd_premul(sd_rgba(r, g, b, a))
#                                   for a in 1..255 (the sanctioned producer; computed by it), 0 at a = 0
#   k    = a * cov                  effective source alpha x 255, 0..65025
#   out_c = R( P_c * cov * 255 + d_c * (65025 - k) )       R(n) = floor((2n + 65025) / 130050)
#   out_a = R(   a * cov * 255 + d_a * (65025 - k) )       = n / 65025 rounded to nearest
#
# ⭐ That is agnos kernel/core/gpu.cyr cov_ref_px — the EXACT rational reference for #92 op 0x02
# GPU_OP_BLEND_COV (8bpp coverage x one uniform premultiplied colour, src-over onto a strided
# surface: this blit's GPU twin) — term for term, with the premultiplied colour sd_premul makes.
# At cov = 255 it collapses (2 * 255 * n + 65025 = 255 * (2n + 255)) to
#   out_c = P_c + floor((2 * d_c * (255 - a) + 255) / 510)
# which is gpu.cyr blend_ref_px, the reference for op 0x01 BLEND_RECT — the op the compositor
# composites this surface WITH. sd_fill_rect_premul computes that form; the suite holds the fill
# and the blit at full coverage to the same cov_ref_px on all 32,896 valid (d_c, d_a) pairs.
#
# ⭐ NO TIES. R hits x.5 only if 2n = 65025 * (2j + 1): even = odd. So round-half-up, round-half-even
# and round-to-nearest are the same function on every input, and the GPU's tie rule never matters.
#
# ⛔ VALIDITY, DERIVED. R is non-decreasing in n. For a valid destination (d_c <= d_a):
#   P_c = floor(c * a / 255) <= a                       (c <= 255)
#   65025 - k >= 0                                      (a, cov <= 255)
#   ⇒ P_c * cov * 255 + d_c * (65025 - k) <= a * cov * 255 + d_a * (65025 - k)   ⇒  out_c <= out_a
#   out_a <= R(k * 255 + 255 * (65025 - k)) = R(255 * 65025) = floor(255.5) = 255
# The same bound holds for out_c with ANY d_c <= 255 (valid or not), so every store is a byte.
# Equality is reached (c = 255, d_c = d_a): the invariant is tight, not merely held.
# Exact endpoints: cov = 0 or a = 0 gives R(d * 65025) = d (the pixel is untouched, and is skipped);
# k = 65025 gives P exactly — (b, g, r, 255) at a = 255, the four bytes sd_canvas_blit_at writes.
# Widest intermediate: 2 * (255 * 65025 + 255 * 65025) + 65025 = 66,390,525 < 2^26.
#
# MEASURED against the kernel (a numpy f32 emulation of kernel/shaders/blend_premul.s and
# blend_cov.s — every v_fma_f32 / v_mul_f32 rounded once to f32, v_cvt_pk_u8_f32 to nearest, both
# tie rules, the literal constants 0xBB808081 / 0x3B808081, exact in 64-bit long double):
#   · op 0x01, the dst term above over ALL 8,421,376 (A, S <= A, d) triples: 0 mismatches.
#   · op 0x02 against this blit, ALL 4,294,967,296 (a, cov, c, d) colour-lane inputs and all
#     16,777,216 (a, cov, d) alpha-lane inputs: worst |difference| 1, on 5,952 + 33 inputs (pack
#     ties to even) or 3,380 + 28 (half up) — the f32 shader against its own exact reference, which
#     is this arithmetic; the kernel's coverage self-test allows and reports exactly that ±1. At
#     cov = 0 and cov = 255 the two agree on every input, as that self-test requires.
#
# ⚠ REPEATED COMPOSITING DRIFTS. One layer is close: over every (c, a, d) the fill is within 1 of
# round(analytic) (MEASURED; worst |out - analytic| 1.49, at c 254, a 1, d 128). Stacking the SAME
# translucent source N times over transparent (MEASURED from the fill form; the single numbers below
# are also asserted through the functions in programs/premul_test.cyr, group L):
#   · ALPHA STALLS below 255 for every a in 1..127, at the smallest d > 255 - 127.5/a, where the dst
#     term d*(255-a)/255 rounds back up to d - a: a = 1 stops at 128 (after 128 layers, having run
#     AHEAD of the analytic 100.8), a = 10 at 243, a = 64 and a = 127 at 254; a >= 128 reaches 255 (8
#     layers at 128). Alpha never decreases. This is 8-bit src-over rounding, bit-identical to #92
#     op 0x01 (the same stack composited on the GPU stalls at the same value), so it is kept.
#   · COLOUR runs mostly LOW, compounding the truncating premultiply: 20 fills of (200, 200, 200) at
#     a = 10 give 0x8D626262 (colour 98, analytic 110.14; alpha 141, analytic 140.43) and 1000 give
#     0xF3A6A6A6 (straight 174 for 200); (128, 128, 128) at a = 1 stalls at 0x80000000, a 50 % BLACK,
#     because P = 0 for every c < 255 at a = 1; 1000 coverage-128 blits of (200, 100, 50) at a = 20
#     settle at 0xF3B34D1A (straight 188, 81, 27). Worst premultiplied-colour error over c in 0..255,
#     a in 8..64, at N = 4 / 8 / 16: -4.44 / -7.79 / -13.95 (up to +1.04 / +1.76 / +3.22). A ROUNDED
#     premultiply, (2*c*a + 255) / 510, would make it two-sided and about 30 % smaller: -2.67..+2.76 /
#     -4.19..+5.26 / -7.56..+9.61 — not removed. Truncation stays because P is then exactly sd_premul,
#     the stack's sanctioned producer, so a pixel sd_premul made and one this module made agree. A
#     client building up an effect from many faint layers should composite once at the summed alpha.
#
# ⚠ AGAINST sd_canvas_blit_at, opaque colour (a = 255) over an OPAQUE destination: the alpha byte is
# 255 from both, and the colour bytes are round-to-nearest here where the straight blit truncates:
#   out_premul = floor((2m + 255) / 510)   vs   out_straight = floor(m / 255),   m = c*cov + d*(255 - cov)
# ⇒ premul - straight is 0 or +1, and +1 exactly when m mod 255 >= 128. MEASURED over all 16,777,216
# (cov, c, d) channel triples: 8,164,890 differ, all by +1; identical for every (c, d) at cov = 0 and
# cov = 255, and for every cov when c == d. programs/premul_test.cyr asserts all of it through both
# functions. (The gap is the straight blit's truncation, not an error here: truncation is what makes
# its 50 % white-over-black read 127.)
#
# MEASURED cost (256x256, best of 5 x 40 passes, this host, ONE idle run; build/ probes —
# perf_solid.cyr and perf_paint.cyr, gitignored like the rest of build/ — not a gate), ns/px.
# ⚠ ns/px figures compare within one run on one host, never across them; these replace the earlier
# table's, which were a different run:
#   full coverage   sd_canvas_blit_at 6.5 | blit_premul a=255 2.6 (one store32) | a=128 10.4
#   mixed coverage  sd_canvas_blit_at 11.9 | blit_premul a=255 10.4 | a=128 10.4
#   rect at a=128   sd_fill_rect_blend 8.5 | sd_fill_rect_premul 8.9
#   clear           sd_clear 8.3 | sd_clear_premul 1.5
#   gradient, full  linear 23.1 straight / 35.1 premul | radial 50.9 / 61.6 | focal 63.7 / 75.6 |
#                   transformed linear 23.2 / 35.6 | transformed radial 56.0 / 66.3
# ⭐ The coverage blit got FASTER when the formula moved into _sd_premul_cov_run — a build of wave 1's
# inlined loop in the same run: a=255 3.4 -> 2.6, a=128 12.1 -> 10.4, mixed 12.1 -> 10.4. (Why was not
# chased; fewer values live across the row is the guess.) Sharing the arithmetic with the paint blit
# cost this loop nothing — but only because the call is per ROW; see the ⛔ on _sd_premul_cov_run.
# ⚠ The gradient rows use a 4-stop ramp with TRANSLUCENT stops, so the STRAIGHT blit is on its blend
# path — paint.cyr's own 14 ns/px linear figure is an opaque ramp, which stores without blending. The
# ~12 ns/px the premultiplied compositor adds is the per-pixel premultiply (three divisions) plus the
# alpha lane the straight blit never computes.
# ================================================================

# P for (color, a) as 0xAARRGGBB: sd_premul's bytes at an explicit a, a clamped to [0, 255], 0 at a <= 0.
fn _sd_premul_color(color, a): i64 {
    if (a <= 0) { return 0; }
    var av = a;
    if (av > 255) { av = 255; }
    return sd_premul(sd_rgba(sd_color_r(color), sd_color_g(color), sd_color_b(color), av));
}

# @public — read pixel (x, y) as 0xAARRGGBB: ALL FOUR BYTES, raw — no legacy alpha mapping (an
# alpha byte of 0 reads 0) and no unpremultiply. Rows by the stride. Out-of-bounds reads return 0,
# which is also transparent black: tell the two apart by the bounds, never by the value.
# (sd_surface_pixel_at, by contrast, drops byte 3.)
fn sd_surface_rgba_at(s, x, y): i64 {
    if (s == 0) { return 0; }
    if (x < 0) { return 0; }
    if (y < 0) { return 0; }
    if (x >= load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET)) { return 0; }
    if (y >= load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET)) { return 0; }
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    return load32(px + y * load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET) + x * 4);
}

# @public — set EVERY pixel of `s` to `color` premultiplied at alpha `a`: bytes
# (floor(b*a/255), floor(g*a/255), floor(r*a/255), a) — exactly sd_premul(sd_rgba(r, g, b, a)).
# `a` <= 0 is FULLY TRANSPARENT (0, 0, 0, 0) whatever the colour; `a` >= 255 is opaque (b, g, r, 255).
# The colour's alpha byte is ignored. Overwrites, does not composite. Rows by the stride; padding
# between rows is untouched. Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for s == 0.
fn sd_clear_premul(s, color, a): i64 {
    if (s == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    var w = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var h = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var stride = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    var p = _sd_premul_color(color, a);
    var y = 0;
    while (y < h) {
        var row = px + y * stride;
        var x = 0;
        while (x < w) {
            store32(row + x * 4, p);
            x = x + 1;
        }
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — composite a w*h rect of `color` at alpha `a` OVER a premultiplied surface, top-left
# (x, y), clipped to the surface on every side:
#   out_c = P_c + floor((2 * d_c * (255 - a) + 255) / 510)   (channels and alpha, P_alpha = a)
# — gpu.cyr blend_ref_px, i.e. #92 op 0x01 with this uniform source (see the header). `a` <= 0
# paints NOTHING; `a` >= 255 stores (b, g, r, 255). w <= 0 or h <= 0 paints nothing (⚠ unlike
# sd_fill_rect, whose span swap paints 2 px for w == 0). The colour's alpha byte is ignored.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for s == 0.
fn sd_fill_rect_premul(s, x, y, w, h, color, a): i64 {
    if (s == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (a <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var sw = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_WIDTH_OFFSET);
    var sh = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_HEIGHT_OFFSET);
    var px = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_PIXELS_OFFSET);
    var stride = load64(s + SD_SURFACE_STRIDE_OFFSET);
    var x0 = x;
    if (x0 < 0) { x0 = 0; }
    var y0 = y;
    if (y0 < 0) { y0 = 0; }
    var x1 = x + w;
    if (x1 > sw) { x1 = sw; }
    var y1 = y + h;
    if (y1 > sh) { y1 = sh; }
    if (x1 <= x0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    if (y1 <= y0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var p = _sd_premul_color(color, a);
    var av = (p >> 24) & 255;
    var pb = p & 255;
    var pg = (p >> 8) & 255;
    var pr = (p >> 16) & 255;
    var ia2 = (255 - av) * 2;
    var yy = y0;
    while (yy < y1) {
        var row = px + yy * stride;
        var xx = x0;
        while (xx < x1) {
            var o = row + xx * 4;
            if (av == 255) {
                store32(o, p);
            } else {
                store8(o, pb + (load8(o) * ia2 + 255) / 510);
                store8(o + 1, pg + (load8(o + 1) * ia2 + 255) / 510);
                store8(o + 2, pr + (load8(o + 2) * ia2 + 255) / 510);
                store8(o + 3, av + (load8(o + 3) * ia2 + 255) / 510);
            }
            xx = xx + 1;
        }
        yy = yy + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ⭐ THE header's cov_ref_px, and the ONE implementation of it in sadish: a RUN of `n` pixels from
# surface offset `o`, their coverage bytes at `cp`, painted with the premultiplied source colour `p`
# (0xAARRGGBB, _sd_premul_color's bytes) whose alpha is `av` and whose channels times 255 are `pb`,
# `pg`, `pr`. Both premultiplied writers that take coverage run through here — sd_canvas_blit_premul_at
# a row at a time, sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at a pixel at a time (its colour changes at every one)
# — so the formula, its rounding and its two endpoints exist once. Every store is a byte and
# out_c <= out_a for a valid destination: proved in the header, swept through BOTH callers in
# programs/premul_test.cyr (F, M) and programs/paint_premul_test.cyr (C, F).
# ⛔ A RUN, NOT A PIXEL, and that is not a style choice: CYRIUS DOES NOT INLINE. A first cut of this
# split called a per-PIXEL helper from the solid blit and MEASURED 16.0 to 17.5 ns/px where the
# inlined loop reads 11.5 to 12.1 (256x256, mixed coverage, the cost table's probe; the spread inside
# each is run-to-run drift, the gap between them is not) — a third to a half more work for a helper
# that loop calls 65,536 times a frame. Per ROW the call vanishes into the row setup: the same probe
# reads 10.4, FASTER than the inlined loop it replaced. The paint blit, at 35 to 105 ns/px, pays it
# per pixel at n = 1 and does not notice.
# ⚠ The endpoints are IN here because they are exits, not cases: cov == 0 is skipped (R(d * 65025) = d
# leaves the pixel exactly as it was) and av * cov == 65025 stores the four bytes of `p` outright,
# which is what the formula collapses to there (130050 P + 65025 over 130050). Neither is a second
# rule, and the suites hold both to this term.
fn _sd_premul_cov_run(o, cp, n, p, pb, pg, pr, av): i64 {
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        var cov = load8(cp + i);
        if (cov != 0) {
            var q = o + i * 4;
            var k = av * cov;
            if (k == 65025) {
                store32(q, p);
            } else {
                var ik = 65025 - k;
                store8(q, ((pb * cov + load8(q) * ik) * 2 + 65025) / 130050);
                store8(q + 1, ((pg * cov + load8(q + 1) * ik) * 2 + 65025) / 130050);
                store8(q + 2, ((pr * cov + load8(q + 2) * ik) * 2 + 65025) / 130050);
                store8(q + 3, ((av * 255 * cov + load8(q + 3) * ik) * 2 + 65025) / 130050);
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_blit_premul_at — composite the canvas coverage onto a PREMULTIPLIED surface with the
# canvas's (0,0) at surface pixel (dx, dy): source = `color` premultiplied at alpha `a`, scaled by
# each pixel's coverage, src-over (the formula and its proof are in the header). (dx, dy) may be
# negative or beyond the surface — clipped on every side exactly as sd_canvas_blit_at clips. Rows by
# sd_surface_stride. A pixel with coverage 0 is not touched; `a` <= 0 paints nothing.
# ⭐ The alpha byte is COMPOSITED, not forced: over a transparent surface an opaque colour leaves
# alpha == coverage, which is what makes an anti-aliased edge an anti-aliased edge under #92.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null cv / surface.
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, surface, color, a, dx, dy): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (surface == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (a <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var cw = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var ch = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var covbuf = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var covstride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    var sw = sd_surface_width(surface);
    var sh = sd_surface_height(surface);
    var spx = sd_surface_pixels(surface);
    var pitch = sd_surface_stride(surface);

    var p = _sd_premul_color(color, a);
    var av = (p >> 24) & 255;
    # P * 255 per channel: the source term is these times the coverage (see _sd_premul_cov_run).
    var pb = (p & 255) * 255;
    var pg = ((p >> 8) & 255) * 255;
    var pr = ((p >> 16) & 255) * 255;

    # Clip the canvas rect [dx, dx+cw) x [dy, dy+ch) to the surface (canvas coordinates).
    var x0 = 0;
    if (dx < 0) { x0 = 0 - dx; }
    var y0 = 0;
    if (dy < 0) { y0 = 0 - dy; }
    var x1 = cw;
    if (dx + x1 > sw) { x1 = sw - dx; }
    var y1 = ch;
    if (dy + y1 > sh) { y1 = sh - dy; }

    # ⛔ BOTH POINTERS ARE ADVANCED TO x0 AND THE RUN STARTS AT INDEX 0 — a run takes a start and a
    # length, not a range, so the clip lives in `+ x0` on each and in `x1 - x0`. The coverage one is
    # the trap: getting it wrong shifts the whole row by a constant, which UNIFORM coverage cannot
    # show and a wrapped-against-packed comparison cannot either (both surfaces shift alike).
    # MEASURED: with that `+ x0` deleted the whole 23-suite sweep stayed green until
    # programs/premul_test.cyr group N (#135, #139, #141, #142, #145-147) was written to read a
    # varying coverage back through an independent per-pixel reference. Move any of these three and
    # group N is what tells you.
    var y = y0;
    while (y < y1) {
        var crow = covbuf + y * covstride + x0;
        var srow = spx + (y + dy) * pitch + (dx + x0) * 4;
        _sd_premul_cov_run(srow, crow, x1 - x0, p, pb, pg, pr, av);
        y = y + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# @public — sd_canvas_blit_premul_at with the canvas at the surface origin.
fn sd_canvas_blit_premul(cv, surface, color, a): i64 {
    return sd_canvas_blit_premul_at(cv, surface, color, a, 0, 0);
}

# One pixel of the gradient blit, and the ONLY place a paint colour is premultiplied: the paint's
# colour `e` at that pixel (0xAARRGGBB with the ACTUAL alpha — a ramp entry, or _sd_gradient_eval's
# value in a cell holding a stop), its coverage byte at `cp`, src-over the premultiplied pixel at `o`
# — a one-pixel run of the formula above. P = _sd_premul_color(e, e's own alpha) = sd_premul(e) for an
# alpha of 1..255, which is every value a ramp entry can hold (the header's ⛔ NO `a` ARGUMENT note).
# ⚠ An alpha byte of 0 — which no ramp entry has — would premultiply to 0 and give k = 0, i.e.
# R(d * 65025) = d: the pixel would be left exactly as it was, never painted opaque. The one place
# byte 3 is read as itself rather than through sd_alpha_of, and it degrades to a no-op, not to the
# additive over-bright ghost a legacy reading would make.
# ⚠ THE PREMULTIPLY IS WRITTEN OUT rather than routed through _sd_premul_color, and the three lines
# below are that function expanded for an alpha already in [0, 255] — floor(c * a / 255) per channel,
# 0 at a = 0, the same bytes sd_premul gives, which is the whole point of the producer. It is expanded
# because CYRIUS DOES NOT INLINE and _sd_premul_color reaches sd_premul -> sd_alpha_of / sd_color_r /
# _g / _b / sd_rgba: TEN calls per painted pixel, MEASURED 67.8 ns/px against 35.1 for the linear
# gradient blit (256x256, full coverage) — the call chain was more than half the blit. ⛔ If the
# module's premultiply ever stops being floor(c * a / 255), these three lines and sd_premul must move
# together; programs/paint_premul_test.cyr computes the expected P from the stops independently and
# would fail, and programs/premul_test.cyr group M holds this path and sd_canvas_blit_premul_at (which
# does call _sd_premul_color) to identical bytes over all 32,896 valid destinations.
fn _sd_premul_paint_px(o, cp, e): i64 {
    var av = (e >> 24) & 255;
    var pb = ((e & 255) * av) / 255;
    var pg = (((e >> 8) & 255) * av) / 255;
    var pr = (((e >> 16) & 255) * av) / 255;
    var p = (av << 24) | (pr << 16) | (pg << 8) | pb;
    return _sd_premul_cov_run(o, cp, 1, p, pb * 255, pg * 255, pr * 255, av);
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at — composite the canvas coverage onto a PREMULTIPLIED surface with
# the canvas's (0,0) at surface pixel (dx, dy), the source colour of each pixel coming from the
# gradient `g` sampled at that pixel's centre in CANVAS space: a gradient panel with anti-aliased
# edges over a transparent window, which sd_canvas_blit_paint_at (straight src-over, dst alpha forced
# to 255) cannot produce and sd_canvas_blit_premul_at (one uniform colour) cannot either.
#
# The paint is src/paint.cyr's — geometry, spread, stops, ramp and the exact per-pixel t are ITS
# contract, read there; every kind is supported (linear, radial, focal radial, any spread, a gradient
# transform), because this is the same loop under a flag. What THIS function fixes is the store:
#   · source alpha = the pixel's INTERPOLATED STRAIGHT stop alpha (1..255), premultiplied per pixel,
#     weighted by the pixel's coverage — the header's cov_ref_px with (a, cov) kept apart;
#   · the destination alpha is COMPOSITED, not forced: over a transparent surface an opaque gradient
#     leaves alpha == coverage, which is what makes the rounded corner a rounded corner under #92;
#   · every output pixel is valid premultiplied (out_c <= out_a) for every valid destination.
# (dx, dy) may be negative or beyond the surface — clipped on every side exactly as the straight
# paint blit clips. Rows by sd_surface_stride. A pixel with coverage 0 is not touched. 0 B per call.
# Returns SADISH_OK (also for a gradient with no stops, or one whose matrix is blocked — both paint
# nothing, as in paint.cyr), or SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for a null cv / surface / g.
# ⚠ Against sd_canvas_blit_paint_at on the same canvas and an opaque destination the RGB bytes can
# differ for two reasons, BOTH the straight blit's: it rounds the effective source alpha DOWN to a
# byte (sa = cov * paint_alpha / 255) and then truncates its store where this one rounds to nearest.
# ⛔ "IDENTICAL AT FULL COVERAGE" IS THE OPAQUE GRADIENT'S PROMISE ONLY — the alpha truncation is
# what makes it, and a translucent stop breaks it:
#   · every stop alpha 255: sa is cov exactly, so at cov = 255 the two blits write byte-identical
#     RGB (asserted pixel by pixel on 12 specs, paint_premul_test group B) and below it
#     premul - straight is 0 or +1 — the ⚠ AGAINST sd_canvas_blit_at note above, unchanged.
#   · a TRANSLUCENT stop: the signed difference premul - straight lies in -2..+2 and REACHES BOTH
#     ENDS. MEASURED over ALL 4,261,478,400 lane inputs (c 0..255, stop alpha 1..255, cov 1..255,
#     d 0..255): 1,403,649,306 differ at all and 3,955,618 by two — +2 at (c 255, alpha 2, cov 193,
#     d 1), straight 1 against 3 here; -2 at (c 55, alpha 4, cov 253, d 225), straight 223 against
#     221. At cov = 255 it narrows to -1..+1 — NOT to 0: (c 0, alpha 1, d 1) is straight 0, here 1.
#     (The exhaustive scan is a build/ probe, gitignored like the perf ones and not a gate; its
#     counts are reproduced through the SHIPPED blits on a 1,560,600-input slice by group L.)
#   Both witnesses and the bound are pinned through both blits in paint_premul_test group L.
# The alpha byte is the real difference: 255 there, composited here.
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at(cv, surface, g, dx, dy): i64 {
    return _sd_paint_blit_run(cv, surface, g, dx, dy, 1);
}

# @public — sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul_at with the canvas at the surface origin.
fn sd_canvas_blit_paint_premul(cv, surface, g): i64 {
    return _sd_paint_blit_run(cv, surface, g, 0, 0, 1);
}
